## 1. SERP Intent Analysis

### 1a. Dominant Content Format

The top-ranking results are a hybrid of two formats: **curated neighborhood guides** (listicle-style with descriptive paragraphs per neighborhood) and **comprehensive overview articles** (long-form editorial pieces organizing neighborhoods by category, geography, or renter type). The brightplace guide already at brightplace.ai/guides/brooklyn-neighborhood-guide demonstrates this format works. Competitors like prospectplaces.com, cohabs.com, junehomes.com, and craigyoskowitz.com all use the curated neighborhood guide format. The Wikipedia result ranks on authority alone, not format. The writing agent should produce a **curated neighborhood guide** organized by renter type or lifestyle category, not a flat alphabetical list or pure listicle.

### 1b. Content Depth Benchmark

Top competitors range from:
- Wikipedia: Reference list only (no narrative depth)
- visitbrooklyn.nyc: Navigation index, minimal copy
- prospectplaces.com: ~3,500–4,500 words with data tables
- cohabs.com: ~1,800 words, neighborhood-by-neighborhood
- junehomes.com: ~5,000+ words (outlier; comprehensive guide format)
- craigyoskowitz.com: ~800 words (brief descriptors)
- newyorkdearest.com: ~2,500 words
- propertyclub.nyc: ~2,800 words

The sweet spot for the /knowledgebase/ format is **1,200–1,500 words**. This is shorter than the major editorial guides but longer than the brief listicles. The article must answer the query completely within this range. It should not attempt to cover every neighborhood; it should cover the neighborhoods renters actually research and use specific, useful data rather than volume.

